we're looking at a situation where  it's even more important that we're  really fast because in voice you have to  be on the ball and you can't have these  awkward pauses  but the steps between uh getting our  models results  and the the customer saying something  there are so many steps between it that  it's causing a lot of  a lot of lag that we didn't expect and  so that's the first issue  the second thing i was wrong about is  what i said i'll use a vendor to  translate the audio just fine  um i had always been under the  impression that since auto captioning  works you know mediocrity that that  speech text wasn't really a solved  problem  it's not most um transcription  models that you see in the marketplace  are built on a  wide band audio so that's six kilohertz  sampling rate  16 kilohertz sampling rate audio and um  that allows for a lot of richness and a  lot of information  that telephone audio doesn't because  telephone audio is limited to an eight  kilohertz sampling rate  this was a great decision for telephones  because ninety percent of the sounds  that actually make up language  are encompassed in that eight kilohertz  band the unfortunate thing  for models is that some very important  uh  sounds aren't actually there so like  when you're on the phone you can't  actually hear i believe it's f and s  sounds because they aren't encompassed  in that band your brain is actually  filling in those gaps for you  which is great because brains are great  at that but models are horrible at  filling in the gaps where we don't give  them data so  our out-of-the-box word error rates from  almost every major  speech transcription provider was almost  40 and so word error rate is how you  measure accuracy  of uh speech recognition or speech to  text and it's basically saying um how  many words did you get wrong  um and there's all sorts of ways to say  if it shifts a little bit how do you  change that calculation it can get very  very interesting and competitive and  nasty  but but by all metrics the  out-of-the-box word error rates for  these models were about 40  which means that our models like our  classification models are  only getting 60 of the information that  they would have otherwise because 40  is basically lost to garbage um and then  we also thought that these models would  serve all of our customers equally  however when we looked at these vendor  provided models from  again literally every big speech text  vendor they they all perform  worse on women and worse on accented  english  this is unacceptable because throughout  america even we have so many different  accents and t-mobile in particular we  have a huge base in georgia  we have a call center in nashville and  these are areas of the united states  with very distinct accents  and if we can't even get our call center  representatives  transcriptions good how will we ever get  the breadth of our customers good  and then we also thought that speed  wouldn't be an issue but we were having  up to five seconds of lag between when a  customer said something when we could  see the transcription  that's quite far behind in a voice  conversation so out of the box models  for telephone audio they suck it's not a  solved problem i was super wrong  i also thought that load would not be an  issue  and so what i mean by that is you know  we're going from having uh  ten percent of the volume that exists in  messaging like ten percent of the  overall care volume to having  a hundred percent of the care volume  which is ninety percent voice  um so the first issue is i thought that  that would scale linearly so i thought  the more confident like for each  conversation we need  each instance of our model could serve a  certain number of conversations and it  wouldn't matter if they were in text or  invoice  this isn't true because voice  conversations are extremely long you are  more likely to meander  to talk about absolute nonsense and  text-based conversations are very very  very business  so it turns out that when this is in  production the number of instances that  we need for 10 times the traffic was  actually  30 times the compute which is it's  very expensive and then there's lots of  moments when you're on the phone where  nobody's saying anything everyone's  called into a call center and had  somebody say let me pull up the details  on your account for you and set there  for a good two minutes and 30 seconds in  complete silence  we were paying people to transcribe  silence so  the load as far as actual conversations  increased but so did the amount of work  that our  models were having to do which was  unanticipated and it's extremely  expensive so we had a few strategies for  how to cut the cost so that way we can  still be viable  uh one very easy build a model to  determine silence and don't transcribe  silence um another strategy to lower the  model cost is that our  our classification models and our intent  models there are these big beautiful  neural network of neural networks like  extremely deep learning uh  our models that are containerized and so  they are a little bit resource intensive  because they  they are they are deep learning models  and so we created a low resource  importance model that after an utterance  is said  we determine whether or not we think  that that is information dense enough to  send to our classification model  the goal here being to filter out some  of the small talk that happens in voice  that doesn't happen in text  to try and increase our overall model  accuracy then we also toyed with  running these models as java services  which we  just got working literally yesterday and  there's a blog in progress and i'm  really excited to talk about that when i  finally can  um but the final assumption i mean this  is the most ridiculous one was that our  models will work just fine  um they don't they they are horrible uh  there are tons of things in casual  speech that casual text does not have  so you have increased filler words you  have back channeling  which is like when you're talking and  somebody said yeah uh-huh  so you have to deal with that you have  to deal with small talk as a concept  which really doesn't happen in business  text  you have to deal with two different  signal environments so we can control  what a call centers could sounds like  but i cannot control where a customer is  calling us from  the first transcription that i had to do  was a 20-minute call that took  multiple days for me to transcribe  because she had dogs barking in the  background  if my human brain can't transcribe text  how could i expect a model to do it and  then even with perfectly human  transcribed audio our overall model  accuracy for our intent  dropped almost 20 percent  so even if that even if it was if the  transcriptions were perfect we would be  losing 20  of our accuracy just because speech is  that much different than text  and these errors compound when you think  about all the transcription errors i was  mentioning so  our the the transcription models we were  spending a ton of money on were only  60 accurate and guarantee a 20  decrease in our accuracy so now we're  left with like some hot garbage to serve  up  um so i was wrong about literally  everything that i thought would make  this product easy uh so what's next  and what's next is we keep building um  we are working with  open source platforms and building our  own in-house models for speech-to-text  which is what i wanted to do from the  very beginning  but by taking 60 days and stitching  together all of these different  technologies and tying them to our text  stuff to build a prototype we were able  to secure enough funding  to start up a team that is actually  going to dig in and build these in-house  voice models of my dreams so we will  have equitable  voice models built on audio that serve  all of our customers equally and are in  our data centers  so that way they will be extremely fast  um and we're going to also work on  serving our models created in r as java  spring boot services  and then just refining the product  vision for the voice space because  in messaging our product was really  focused on context switching we had you  know a messaging agent might have six or  seven different windows open and they're  popping back and forth between all these  conversations but in voice  you're focused on one person's issues so  our problem space becomes different  where we make our bread and butter in  the voice spaces by eliminating all that  dead air  and so even though the results were  horrible  and all of my assumptions were  ridiculous we were able to prove that it  worked enough to get  millions and millions of dollars in  funding to do what i what i really want  to do and it's create beautiful models  in-house  and so in short what one does not simply  lift and shift to text and i'll be a  product to streaming voice and if you  are looking to do that  i hope that i've illuminated some of the  the stumbling blocks that will very very  obviously be in your way because it's  not as straightforward as it  as it seems but even with all of that  degradation we were able to prove real  value  and so the flip side of this is maybe  sometimes it's really okay to buy a bad  vendor product for a little while  if it gets you funding to build your own  in the end um and with that i would like  to say  thank you um everything that i've  learned in this entire project is really  from a machine learning engineer on my  team called phoebe parsons who has a  background in speech  so she was the person all around telling  me no heather don't go into the weeds  don't go in the weeds pulling me back  but still  there were so many little assumptions  that are made you think that this is  very simple  a simple simple technological problem  that doesn't involve a lot of modeling i  thought we had already built the models  and it turns out i was wrong we haven't  already built the models we have way  way way way more models to build now  with that thank you  um and if you want to talk to me i'm  extremely active on twitter but if you  want like fast business responses my  my work email is definitely the one to  take thanks
 

